  "total_cost_usd": 0.2976665,
  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic**, with confidence 0.9. The learner's cost tracks how similar a task's mapping is to what it was trained on, not how many of its rules are new.\n\n**Design:** I trained on two tasks, L1-S1-M1 and L3-S2-M4. I then tested two kinds of task, chosen so the two hypotheses predict opposite results:\n- **Recombinations:** L3-S1-M1 and L1-S2-M4. All their rules were already trained, but their mappings share 0% of responses with both training tasks. Part-based predicts these are cheap; holistic predicts they are expensive.\n- **New rules, similar mappings:** L1-S1-M2 has one new rule and is 75% similar to a trained task. L1-S4-M2 has two new rules and is 56% similar. Part-based predicts these are expensive; holistic predicts they are cheap.\n\n**Results** (the tests are very noisy, with single results ranging from 1 to 68):\n\n| Group | Tests | Mean examples to criterion |\n|---|---|---|\n| Recombinations (0 new rules, 0% similar) | 23 | ~43 |\n| New rules, high similarity | 22 | ~25 |\n| L4-S4-M2 (3 new rules, 19% similar) | 5 | ~38 |\n\nThe gap between the first two groups is about 3.5 standard errors, and it runs the opposite way to what part-based predicts. As an extra check, L4-S4-M2, where all three rules are new, was still cheaper on average than the recombinations whose rules were all familiar. That also fits holistic and not part-based, though it rests on only 5 tests.\n\nI used 52 of the 60 budget: 2 to train and 50 to test.",
